  <data name="HomeTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Accessible Blazor components under a strict security policy</value></data>
  <data name="HomeLead" xml:space="preserve"><value>A component library built for applications that forbid inline style, require accessibility, and want a theme they can drive end to end.</value></data>
  <data name="HomeSeeComponents" xml:space="preserve"><value>See the components</value></data>
  <data name="HomeSeeCustomize" xml:space="preserve"><value>Customise the theme</value></data>
  <data name="HomePillarsTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>What sets the library apart</value></data>
  <data name="PillarCspTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Strict security policy</value></data>
  <data name="PillarCspBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>No inline style attribute, no injected style element, no dynamic script evaluation. The library runs under a policy that refuses all of it.</value></data>
  <data name="PillarThemeTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Fully tokenised theme</value></data>
  <data name="PillarThemeBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>Colours, type, spacing, radii, shadows and the chart palette all go through CSS variables, with no value frozen inside a rule.</value></data>
  <data name="PillarA11yTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Accessibility backed by tests</value></data>
  <data name="PillarA11yBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>Roles, states and labels are checked by the test suite, not merely promised by the documentation.</value></data>
  <data name="PillarWeightTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Weight kept in check</value></data>
  <data name="PillarWeightBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>Continuous integration checks that the shipped stylesheet is the minified copy, and measures the assembly and the NuGet package against a size budget.</value></data>
  <data name="DocsPageTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Documentation</value></data>
  <data name="DocsTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Documentation</value></data>
  <data name="DocsLead" xml:space="preserve"><value>From installing the package to your first custom theme, in five steps.</value></data>
  <data name="DocsInstallTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Install the package</value></data>
  <data name="DocsInstallBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>The library is referenced like any other NuGet package.</value></data>
  <data name="DocsRegisterTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Register the services</value></data>
  <data name="DocsRegisterBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>A single call registers everything the components need.</value></data>
  <data name="DocsCssTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Reference the stylesheet</value></data>
  <data name="DocsCssBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>The stylesheet ships as a static asset of the package.</value></data>
  <data name="DocsCspTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Security policy</value></data>
  <data name="DocsCspBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>The library asks for nothing beyond these directives: no inline style, no dynamic script evaluation.</value></data>
  <data name="DocsThemeTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Customise the theme</value></data>
  <data name="DocsThemeBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>Redefining one variable repaints every component that reads it.</value></data>
  <data name="DocsThemePointer" xml:space="preserve"><value>To compose a full theme without writing CSS:</value></data>
